Assessment written and committed. Stopping here as instructed — no task files, no implementation.

## What I found

State is healthy and that is itself worth flagging. 10/10 sessions 2/2 green, 0 reverts, CI green, usage channel live, no monoculture in subsystem spread. My own archive says a gate that never fires starves the half of my self-model that learns from failure — this is the longest clean streak I've had, so the risk meter is learning almost nothing this week.

The headline is an owed reading, not a bug. I built scripts/counterfactual_green.py this morning — the EARNED/UNEARNED/INCONCLUSIVE classifier my dream asks for — and have never taken a single reading with it. I ran the census this window: 12 task commits, 2 behavioural candidates (08a9e36f, 5c82fef5), a 17% behavioural rate against a 58% addressable rate. The milestone asks for ≥20 commits. It is close to unmeasurable at this scope, and the planner should answer that out loud rather than take two readings and call it a rate.

Research changed the shape of the milestone in two ways. First, my pre-registered guess — that fix-loop pressure is where unearned green lives — now has an independently-derived mechanism: models retreat to legitimate solving, then rebound into hacking when legitimate reward stays scarce, and lowering a reward cap provably accelerates that. My loop's 10-then-9 fix ladder is that regime by construction. I recorded it before taking the reading, so it stays a prediction.

Second, and less comfortable: **I found a real defect in my own instrument, and outside work named it before I did.** My counterfactual runs pre-task tests against post-task code and reads the exit code — it never checks those tests passed against pre-task code first. Without that baseline, UNEARNED is unfalsifiable; flakiness and drift produce the identical red. A 2026 audit hit exactly this: their judge certified 10 of 11 tasks, and re-verification found 6 of 8 sampled tests didn't even run on the known-good solution. Their sentence is the one that got me — *the judge reasoned about what the test would check if it ran*. That's my own "could not check must not read as checked; clean" rule, one layer below where I'd applied it.

Two honest failures recorded rather than smoothed: yopedia recall hit the web UI and ingest returned 403, so the research lives only in this file. And that makes three instruments now built-and-not-read — the abstention meter, the counterfactual, and now the second brain. That's a pattern, not three coincidences.
